---
import TextbookCallout from '../TextbookCallout.astro';
import TextbookChunk from '../TextbookChunk.astro';
import TextbookPageIntro from '../TextbookPageIntro.astro';
import TextbookSectionBlock from '../TextbookSectionBlock.astro';
import type { TextbookChapterModel, TextbookSectionModel } from '../../../lib/textbook-model';

interface Props {
  chapter: TextbookChapterModel;
}

const { chapter } = Astro.props as Props;

const requireSection = (code: string): TextbookSectionModel => {
  const section = chapter.sections.find((candidate) => candidate.code === code);
  if (!section) {
    throw new Error(`Missing textbook section "${code}" for rebuilt chapter ${chapter.code}.`);
  }
  return section;
};

const a41 = requireSection('A4.1');
const a42 = requireSection('A4.2');
const a43 = requireSection('A4.3');
const a44 = requireSection('A4.4');
---

<TextbookPageIntro code={chapter.code} title={chapter.title} summary={chapter.summary} visibility={chapter.visibility}>
  <p>
    A4 begins by distinguishing the main forms of machine learning and the hardware contexts in
    which they are deployed. At HL, the focus moves into preprocessing and model comparison so
    students can judge not only what a model does, but also why it succeeds or fails.
  </p>
  <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Reading pattern">
    <p>
      First identify how the system learns: from labelled data, from unlabelled data, from reward,
      or by adapting an existing model. Then ask whether the data is clean enough and the hardware
      suitable enough before comparing specific HL approaches or discussing ethical impact.
    </p>
  </TextbookCallout>
</TextbookPageIntro>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a41}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A4.1.1"
    summary="Describe the main machine-learning types by linking each one to its training pattern, output, and real-world application."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Artificial intelligence is the broader field; machine learning is the part of that field that
      learns patterns from data rather than relying only on fixed rules. The first distinction to make
      is how the learning happens, because that determines what kind of task the model can handle.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-machine-learning-types">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Type</th>
          <th scope="col">How it learns</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical output</th>
          <th scope="col">Example application</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Supervised learning</td>
          <td>Uses labelled input-output examples</td>
          <td>Predicted value or predicted class</td>
          <td>Spam filtering, grade prediction, medical risk scoring</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Unsupervised learning</td>
          <td>Finds structure in unlabelled data</td>
          <td>Clusters, associations, or patterns</td>
          <td>Customer segmentation, recommendation support, basket analysis</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reinforcement learning</td>
          <td>Learns through trial, feedback, and reward</td>
          <td>Improved action policy</td>
          <td>Game agents, robotics, route optimization</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Deep learning</td>
          <td>Uses multi-layer neural networks to learn complex features</td>
          <td>High-dimensional pattern recognition</td>
          <td>Image classification, speech processing, generative systems</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Transfer learning</td>
          <td>Adapts an already trained model to a related task</td>
          <td>Faster improvement on a new but similar problem</td>
          <td>Specializing a generic image model for plant-disease detection</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Common misconception">
      <p>
        Deep learning is not separate from machine learning. It is one approach within machine
        learning, distinguished by the use of neural-network layers.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>

    <h3 id="worked-example-matching-machine-learning-types">Worked example: matching a learning type to a task</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
          <th scope="col">Best fit</th>
          <th scope="col">Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Predict a student's final score from previous assessment data</td>
          <td>Supervised learning</td>
          <td>The target is known in past data, so the model can learn from labelled examples.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Group music listeners by behaviour without predefined categories</td>
          <td>Unsupervised learning</td>
          <td>The system must discover structure rather than predict from given labels.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Teach a robot to improve movement over repeated attempts</td>
          <td>Reinforcement learning</td>
          <td>The robot adjusts future actions using reward from past outcomes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Adapt a large image model to identify a specific flower species</td>
          <td>Transfer learning</td>
          <td>An existing model already knows useful image features, so only limited retraining is needed.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A4.1.2"
    summary="Describe hardware requirements by matching platform and processor choices to training scale, latency, and deployment context."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Hardware choice depends on the task. Small educational models, large cloud-trained models,
      and low-latency edge systems do not need the same balance of CPU, GPU, accelerator,
      memory, and network access.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-machine-learning-hardware-contexts">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Platform</th>
          <th scope="col">Best use</th>
          <th scope="col">Main strength</th>
          <th scope="col">Main constraint</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Standard laptop with CPU</td>
          <td>Learning, prototyping, small models</td>
          <td>Accessible and flexible</td>
          <td>Limited speed, memory, and storage for large training jobs</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Dedicated workstation with GPU</td>
          <td>Medium-scale training and experimentation</td>
          <td>Parallel processing for matrix-heavy workloads</td>
          <td>Higher local hardware cost and power demand</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Edge device with accelerator</td>
          <td>Real-time local inference</td>
          <td>Low latency and less data transfer</td>
          <td>Limited model size and hardware flexibility</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Cloud GPU or TPU</td>
          <td>Large training and scalable deployment</td>
          <td>Elastic access to specialist compute</td>
          <td>Provider dependency, data-transfer concerns, and continuing cost</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>High-performance computing centre</td>
          <td>Research-scale work</td>
          <td>Very large compute capacity</td>
          <td>Restricted access and less day-to-day convenience</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-choosing-hardware-for-deployment">Worked example: choosing hardware for deployment</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
          <th scope="col">Recommended setup</th>
          <th scope="col">Reason</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Classroom investigation using a small tabular data set</td>
          <td>Laptop CPU, with optional entry-level GPU</td>
          <td>The workload is modest, so accessibility matters more than maximum speed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Image model trained on a very large shared data set</td>
          <td>Cloud GPU or TPU</td>
          <td>Training needs specialist parallel hardware and the ability to scale.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Safety system running continuously inside a vehicle</td>
          <td>Edge device</td>
          <td>Decisions must be local and fast even if network access is unstable.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a42}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A4.2.1"
    summary="Describe why data cleaning matters, because models learn from poor-quality data as readily as they learn from useful patterns."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Data cleaning is significant because machine-learning systems do not automatically recognize
      that a value is duplicated, misformatted, missing, or implausible. If the training data is poor,
      the model can learn the wrong pattern with confidence.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-data-cleaning-actions">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Data issue</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical response</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Missing values</td>
          <td>Incomplete cases weaken pattern detection</td>
          <td>Impute carefully or remove records when justified</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Duplicate records</td>
          <td>Over-represented examples can skew the model</td>
          <td>Deduplicate exact or near-duplicate cases</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Incorrect or inconsistent formats</td>
          <td>Dates, units, or labels may be interpreted wrongly</td>
          <td>Standardize formats before training</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Outliers</td>
          <td>Extreme values may distort the learned relationship</td>
          <td>Investigate, cap, transform, or remove when invalid</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Irrelevant features</td>
          <td>Noise adds complexity without useful predictive value</td>
          <td>Filter them out before or during modelling</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-cleaning-a-school-dataset">Worked example: cleaning a school data set</h3>
    <p>
      Imagine a model predicting whether students may need additional support. The raw table
      contains duplicate attendance rows, missing homework counts, and one impossible sleep value
      of 52 hours per day. Leaving those values in place risks teaching the model patterns that do
      not reflect real student behaviour.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cleaning would remove the duplicate, flag or correct the impossible value, and deal with the
      missing homework field using a justified method rather than ignoring the problem. The result
      is not perfect data, but it is far more reliable for training.
    </p>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Common misconception">
      <p>
        More rows do not automatically make a better model. A larger data set full of duplicates,
        missing values, or invalid records can reduce accuracy rather than improve it.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A4.2.2, A4.2.3"
    summary="Describe how feature selection and dimensionality reduction help a model stay focused, efficient, and generalizable."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Feature selection asks which input variables are actually useful. Dimensionality reduction asks
      whether the overall feature space has become so large that patterns are harder to find, harder to
      visualize, and more expensive to process.
    </p>

    <h3 id="core-understanding-feature-selection-and-dimensionality">Core understanding</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Idea</th>
          <th scope="col">What it does</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Filter methods</td>
          <td>Score features statistically before training</td>
          <td>Fast way to remove obviously weak features</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Wrapper methods</td>
          <td>Compare different feature subsets with a model</td>
          <td>Can improve fit, but costs more time and compute</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Embedded methods</td>
          <td>Select features during model training</td>
          <td>Combines selection with learning</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Dimensionality reduction</td>
          <td>Reduces the number of effective variables</td>
          <td>Can limit sparsity, simplify patterns, and lower hardware demand</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      Too many features can create the curse of dimensionality: the data becomes so spread out across
      many dimensions that meaningful generalizations are harder to form. This is why a smaller,
      better-chosen feature set can outperform a larger and noisier one.
    </p>

    <h3 id="worked-example-reducing-a-feature-set">Worked example: reducing a feature set</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Original feature</th>
          <th scope="col">Keep, reduce, or remove?</th>
          <th scope="col">Reason</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Average homework completion</td>
          <td>Keep</td>
          <td>Directly related to the predicted educational outcome</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Three separate but near-identical attendance columns</td>
          <td>Reduce</td>
          <td>They may capture the same pattern repeatedly</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Student ID number</td>
          <td>Remove</td>
          <td>Acts as an identifier rather than a meaningful predictor</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Thirty detailed questionnaire items with overlapping meaning</td>
          <td>Reduce</td>
          <td>Too much detail can increase sparsity and weaken generalization</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      Techniques such as PCA and LDA may be used in practice, but the syllabus emphasis here is on
      understanding why reduction matters, not on deriving the mathematics behind those methods.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a43}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A4.3.1, A4.3.2"
    summary="Explain the difference between supervised prediction of continuous outcomes and supervised prediction of discrete categories."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Supervised learning uses labelled examples, but the nature of the label matters. Linear
      regression predicts a continuous value, while classification techniques predict which category an
      item belongs to.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-supervised-prediction-types">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Task type</th>
          <th scope="col">Target output</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical techniques</th>
          <th scope="col">Example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Linear regression</td>
          <td>Continuous numerical value</td>
          <td>Line of best fit across labelled data</td>
          <td>Predicting rent, temperature, or final score</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Classification</td>
          <td>Discrete category</td>
          <td>K-nearest neighbours, decision trees, neural classifiers</td>
          <td>Spam vs not spam, disease vs no disease, image class</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      A regression model can be summarized as a rule such as <code>prediction = intercept + slope x input</code>.
      The key idea is not the algebra itself, but that the model explains a relationship well enough to
      estimate unseen continuous outcomes. Classification techniques instead construct a decision rule
      that assigns a new case to a category.
    </p>

    <h3 id="worked-example-continuous-vs-categorical-prediction">Worked example: continuous vs categorical prediction</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Problem</th>
          <th scope="col">Best approach</th>
          <th scope="col">Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Estimate a house price from size and location factors</td>
          <td>Linear regression</td>
          <td>The output is numerical and varies across a continuous range.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Decide whether an email is spam</td>
          <td>Classification</td>
          <td>The output is one category from a fixed set.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Identify whether a flower sample belongs to species A, B, or C</td>
          <td>Classification</td>
          <td>The task is to assign a class, not a measurement.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A4.3.3"
    summary="Explain how evaluation metrics and hyperparameter tuning help compare supervised models without relying on accuracy alone."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Hyperparameter tuning adjusts settings chosen before training, such as the number of neighbours
      in KNN, the depth of a decision tree, or the learning rate in a neural network. Tuning matters
      because different settings can change whether a model underfits, overfits, or generalizes well.
    </p>

    <h3 id="core-understanding-evaluation-and-tuning">Core understanding</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Measure or setting</th>
          <th scope="col">What it tells you</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Accuracy</td>
          <td>Overall proportion of correct predictions</td>
          <td>Useful starting point, but can hide uneven performance</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Precision</td>
          <td>How many positive predictions were actually correct</td>
          <td>Important when false positives are costly</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Recall</td>
          <td>How many actual positives were successfully found</td>
          <td>Important when false negatives are costly</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>F1 score</td>
          <td>Balances precision and recall</td>
          <td>Useful when both error types matter</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Hyperparameter choice</td>
          <td>Controls model behaviour before training</td>
          <td>Can reduce overfitting or improve generalization</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-choosing-a-hyperparameter">Worked example: choosing a hyperparameter</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">K value</th>
          <th scope="col">Accuracy</th>
          <th scope="col">Recall</th>
          <th scope="col">F1</th>
          <th scope="col">Interpretation</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>1</td>
          <td>0.94</td>
          <td>0.71</td>
          <td>0.79</td>
          <td>Very sensitive to local noise; possible overfitting</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>5</td>
          <td>0.92</td>
          <td>0.83</td>
          <td>0.84</td>
          <td>Better balance between stability and sensitivity</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>15</td>
          <td>0.88</td>
          <td>0.76</td>
          <td>0.78</td>
          <td>Decision boundary may now be too smooth</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Common misconception">
      <p>
        The highest accuracy does not automatically mean the best model. A model may appear strong
        overall while still failing badly on the class that matters most in the scenario.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A4.3.4, A4.3.5"
    summary="Describe how unsupervised methods uncover groupings and associations without needing labelled answers."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Unsupervised learning does not predict against a known target. Instead, it searches for structure
      already present in the data, such as similar groups or frequently linked attributes.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-unsupervised-pattern-discovery">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Technique</th>
          <th scope="col">What it produces</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical use</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Clustering</td>
          <td>Groups of similar data points</td>
          <td>Customer segmentation, social-group analysis, anomaly grouping</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Association rule learning</td>
          <td>Rules showing attributes that often appear together</td>
          <td>Market-basket analysis, recommendation support, pattern discovery</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      In association rule learning, support measures how often a pattern appears in the whole data
      set, while confidence measures how often the consequent appears when the antecedent appears.
      These values help judge whether a discovered rule is worth paying attention to.
    </p>

    <h3 id="worked-example-unsupervised-insights">Worked example: unsupervised insights</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
          <th scope="col">Technique</th>
          <th scope="col">Insight produced</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Retailer analysing purchase histories</td>
          <td>Association rules</td>
          <td>Customers who buy pasta frequently also buy sauce in the same basket.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Streaming platform exploring user behaviour</td>
          <td>Clustering</td>
          <td>Listeners naturally split into groups with different usage patterns.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A4.3.6, A4.3.7"
    summary="Describe reinforcement learning and genetic algorithms as two different ways of improving decisions or solutions over time."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Reinforcement learning improves behaviour through interaction with an environment and a reward
      signal. Genetic algorithms improve candidate solutions across generations using selection,
      crossover, and mutation. Both are adaptive, but they solve problems in different ways.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-reward-and-evolution">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Approach</th>
          <th scope="col">How improvement happens</th>
          <th scope="col">Strong application</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Reinforcement learning</td>
          <td>Agent tries actions and adjusts policy from reward feedback</td>
          <td>Robotics, games, dynamic routing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Genetic algorithms</td>
          <td>Population of solutions evolves through fitness-based selection</td>
          <td>Timetabling, route optimization, design search</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-reward-vs-evolution">Worked example: reward vs evolution</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Problem</th>
          <th scope="col">Better match</th>
          <th scope="col">Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Warehouse robot learning safer paths around moving obstacles</td>
          <td>Reinforcement learning</td>
          <td>The agent can improve through repeated action, state, and reward cycles.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>School timetable with many competing scheduling constraints</td>
          <td>Genetic algorithm</td>
          <td>Candidate timetables can be compared, combined, and mutated to improve fitness.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A4.3.8, A4.3.9"
    summary="Outline ANN structure and describe why CNNs are especially effective for image data."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Artificial neural networks use interconnected layers of nodes to model complex patterns. A
      multi-layer network can represent relationships that are difficult to capture with a simple line or
      tree. Convolutional neural networks are a specialized form designed to learn spatial structure in
      images.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-neural-network-structures">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Model</th>
          <th scope="col">Main structure</th>
          <th scope="col">What it is good at</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>ANN</td>
          <td>Input layer, one or more hidden layers, output layer</td>
          <td>Capturing complex relationships in general feature data</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>CNN</td>
          <td>Convolution, activation, pooling, then dense layers</td>
          <td>Learning edges, textures, shapes, and higher-level image features</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-image-processing-hierarchy">Worked example: image-processing hierarchy</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">CNN stage</th>
          <th scope="col">Role</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Early convolution layers</td>
          <td>Detect simple local features such as edges and corners</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Later convolution layers</td>
          <td>Combine simpler features into shapes and object parts</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Final dense layers</td>
          <td>Turn learned features into the final classification or prediction</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A4.3.10"
    summary="Explain model selection and comparison by weighing performance against context, constraints, and interpretability."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Model selection is not a search for the single most fashionable algorithm. It is a reasoned choice
      based on data type, performance, latency, interpretability, and hardware cost. Model comparison
      matters because two systems can perform differently under real deployment constraints even when
      they are trained on the same problem.
    </p>

    <h3 id="core-understanding-model-selection">Core understanding</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Factor</th>
          <th scope="col">Question to ask</th>
          <th scope="col">Effect on choice</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Data type</td>
          <td>Is the data tabular, text, image, or mixed?</td>
          <td>Different models suit different structures</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Performance</td>
          <td>Which metrics matter in this context?</td>
          <td>Accuracy alone may not be enough</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Latency</td>
          <td>How fast must the prediction be produced?</td>
          <td>Slow but accurate models may fail deployment needs</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Interpretability</td>
          <td>Must a human explain the decision?</td>
          <td>Highly regulated contexts may prefer simpler models</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Compute cost</td>
          <td>What hardware is available for training and inference?</td>
          <td>Resource-heavy models may be impractical</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-comparing-three-models">Worked example: comparing three models</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Model</th>
          <th scope="col">Validation score</th>
          <th scope="col">Latency</th>
          <th scope="col">Interpretability</th>
