  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a44}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A4.4.1"
    summary="Discuss ethical implications by weighing technical benefits against human consequences, responsibility, and rights."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Ethical discussion in machine learning requires more than naming a risk. Strong responses weigh
      different stakeholders, explain how harm could arise, and consider how technical design and
      social impact interact.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-machine-learning-ethics">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Issue</th>
          <th scope="col">How it appears</th>
          <th scope="col">Possible consequence</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Accountability</td>
          <td>Unclear responsibility when an automated decision causes harm</td>
          <td>Victims struggle to challenge or appeal outcomes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Fairness and bias</td>
          <td>Skewed training data or design choices disadvantage some groups</td>
          <td>Unfair hiring, lending, or policing decisions</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Consent and privacy</td>
          <td>Data is collected or reused without informed permission</td>
          <td>Loss of control over personal information</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Security</td>
          <td>Models are attacked through poisoning, evasion, or inversion</td>
          <td>Manipulated outputs or exposure of sensitive data</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Environmental impact</td>
          <td>Large training runs use substantial energy</td>
          <td>Higher resource consumption and carbon cost</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Transparency</td>
          <td>Users cannot easily see why a system made a decision</td>
          <td>Reduced trust and weaker accountability</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-ethical-analysis-of-automated-recruitment">Worked example: ethical analysis of automated recruitment</h3>
    <p>
      An automated recruitment system may process applications quickly and reduce repetitive
      workload for staff. However, if historical training data contains bias, the system can reproduce or
      amplify unfair patterns while appearing objective.
    </p>
    <p>
      A balanced discussion would consider the employer, applicants, software provider, and the
      people responsible for auditing the data and outputs. The question is not only whether the tool is
      efficient, but whether it remains fair, explainable, and open to challenge.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A4.4.2"
    summary="Discuss why ethics must be reassessed as computer technologies become more deeply integrated into daily life."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Ethical rules cannot stay fixed while technology keeps changing. As systems move from optional
      tools to everyday infrastructure, questions of privacy, equity, control, and wellbeing become
      broader and more persistent.
    </p>

    <h3 id="core-understanding-technology-integration-and-ethics">Core understanding</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Integration area</th>
          <th scope="col">Opportunity</th>
          <th scope="col">Ethical tension</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Pervasive AI services</td>
          <td>Continuous personalization and automation</td>
          <td>Surveillance and constant data extraction</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Recommendation systems</td>
          <td>More relevant content and services</td>
          <td>Echo chambers, manipulation, and narrowed viewpoints</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>AR and VR</td>
          <td>Richer simulation, learning, and interaction</td>
          <td>Behavioural tracking and effects on perception or wellbeing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Public-space analytics</td>
          <td>Traffic planning, safety monitoring, service optimization</td>
          <td>Consent, proportionality, and civil-liberty concerns</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Personal data ecosystems</td>
          <td>Convenient linked services across devices</td>
          <td>Unclear ownership and control of personal data</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-reassessing-city-scale-technology">Worked example: reassessing city-scale technology</h3>
    <p>
      A city may combine transport apps, camera analytics, and personalised alerts to improve traffic
      flow and service access. The same system may also expand tracking of movement, create unequal
      effects across communities, and make it harder for people to understand how their data is used.
    </p>

    <TextbookCallout tone="recap" label="Discussion rule">
      <p>
        For A4.4, discussion should compare benefits and risks, identify stakeholders, and explain why
        ethical review must continue as technologies and social conditions change.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>
